`vsetvli`, `vsetivli`, and `vsetvl` configure `vl` (vector length) and `vtype` (vector type) for strip-mining loops. All three write the effective `vl` to `rd` (typically `x0` to discard or a GPR to save for the loop body). The AVL (application vector length) specifies the desired elements to process; hardware returns `vl` ≤ AVL limited by VLMAX (derived from SEW/LMUL). `vtype` encodes SEW (element width), LMUL (vector register grouping), and tail/mask policies (ta/tu, ma/mu).

**Assembly formats:**

```
vsetvli  rd, rs1, vtypei       # AVL in rs1, vtype from immediate fields
vsetivli rd, uimm5, vtypei     # AVL as 5-bit immediate (0-31)
vsetvl   rd, rs1, rs2          # AVL in rs1, vtype in rs2
```

`vtypei` immediate: `vsetvli rd, rs1, e8|e16|e32|e64, m1|m2|m4|m8|mf2|mf4|mf8, ta|tu, ma|mu`

**Intrinsics:**

```c
// vsetvli forms
size_t __riscv_vsetvl_e8m1(size_t avl);   // plus all SEW/LMUL combos: e8m2..e64m8
size_t __riscv_vsetvl_e8m1_ta(size_t avl);
size_t __riscv_vsetvl_e8m1_tu(size_t avl);
size_t __riscv_vsetvl_e8m1_tama(size_t avl);
size_t __riscv_vsetvl_e8m1_tamu(size_t avl);
size_t __riscv_vsetvl_e8m1_tuma(size_t avl);
size_t __riscv_vsetvl_e8m1_tumu(size_t avl);

// vsetivli forms (immediate AVL)
size_t __riscv_vsetivli_e8m1(unsigned avl);  // avl must be 0-31, same suffix variants

// vsetvl form (dynamic vtype)
size_t __riscv_vsetvl(size_t avl, size_t vtype);
size_t __riscv_vsetvlmax(size_t vtype);      // AVL = ~0, returns VLMAX
```

`vtype` encoding for `vsetvl`: `vtype = (sew << 3) | (lmul & 7) | (ta << 6) | (ma << 7)`, use `__riscv_vundefined_v_*()` helpers or manual construction.

`vle32.v` loads 32-bit elements into a vector register; use when SEW=32 or when you need 32-bit data regardless of current SEW. `vse16.v` stores 16-bit elements from a vector register; use when SEW=16 or when storing 16-bit data.

**Formats:**
```
vle32.v vd, (rs1), v0.t   # unit-stride load, EEW=32
vse16.v vs3, (rs1), v0.t  # unit-stride store, EEW=16
```
- `vd`: destination vector register (load)
- `vs3`: source vector register (store)
- `(rs1)`: base address in scalar register
- `v0.t`: optional mask (tied to v0, tail-agnostic)
- `<eew>`: explicit element width (32 for vle32, 16 for vse16)

**Intrinsics:**
```c
// vle32.v
vint32m1_t __riscv_vle32_v_i32m1(const int32_t *rs1, size_t vl);
vfloat32m1_t __riscv_vle32_v_f32m1(const float *rs1, size_t vl);

// vse16.v
void __riscv_vse16_v_i16m1(int16_t *rs1, vint16m1_t vs3, size_t vl);
void __riscv_vse16_v_f16m1(_Float16 *rs1, vfloat16m1_t vs3, size_t vl);
```

`vlsseg2e32.v` loads 2 segments of 32-bit elements with stride; `vssseg3e16.v` stores 3 segments of 16-bit elements with stride. `rs2` holds the byte stride between the start of consecutive segments (not between elements).

Assembly formats:
```
vlsseg2e32.v vd, (rs1), rs2, vm   # vd, vd+1 get segment 0,1; rs1=base, rs2=stride
vssseg3e16.v vs3, (rs1), rs2, vm  # vs3, vs3+1, vs3+2 store segments; rs1=base, rs2=stride
```

Intrinsics:
```
// vlsseg2e32.v: 2 segments, 32-bit, stride
vint32m1x2_t __riscv_vlsseg2e32_v_i32m1x2(const int32_t *base, ptrdiff_t stride, size_t vl);
vuint32m1x2_t __riscv_vlsseg2e32_v_u32m1x2(const uint32_t *base, ptrdiff_t stride, size_t vl);
vfloat32m1x2_t __riscv_vlsseg2e32_v_f32m1x2(const float *base, ptrdiff_t stride, size_t vl);

// vssseg3e16.v: 3 segments, 16-bit, stride
void __riscv_vssseg3e16_v_i16m1x3(int16_t *base, ptrdiff_t stride, vint16m1x3_t v_tuple, size_t vl);
void __riscv_vssseg3e16_v_u16m1x3(uint16_t *base, ptrdiff_t stride, vuint16m1x3_t v_tuple, size_t vl);
void __riscv_vssseg3e16_v_f16m1x3(_Float16 *base, ptrdiff_t stride, vfloat16m1x3_t v_tuple, size_t vl);
```

These instructions perform add-with-carry and subtract-with-borrow, taking the carry/borrow-in from `v0[0]` (masked bit of vector register `v0`). The `.vvm` variants take two vector operands, `.vxm` take a vector and scalar (x-reg), and `.vim` take a vector and immediate. Results are always **widened to 2*SEW bits**: the lower SEW bits go to the destination vector register, and the upper SEW bits (carry/borrow-out) go to `v0` as a mask.

**Assembly formats:**
```
vadc.vvm vd, vs2, vs1, v0  # vd[i] = vs2[i] + vs1[i] + v0.mask[i]
vadc.vxm vd, vs2, rs1, v0  # vd[i] = vs2[i] + x[rs1] + v0.mask[i]
vadc.vim vd, vs2, imm, v0  # vd[i] = vs2[i] + imm + v0.mask[i]

vsbc.vvm vd, vs2, vs1, v0  # vd[i] = vs2[i] - vs1[i] - v0.mask[i]
vsbc.vxm vd, vs2, rs1, v0  # vd[i] = vs2[i] - x[rs1] - v0.mask[i]
```

**RVV C intrinsics:**
```c
// vadc - result in tuple (carry_out, sum)
vint8m1_t __riscv_vadc_vvm_i8m1(vint8m1_t vs2, vint8m1_t vs1, vbool8_t carryin, size_t vl);
vint8m1_t __riscv_vadc_vxm_i8m1(vint8m1_t vs2, int8_t rs1, vbool8_t carryin, size_t vl);
vint8m1_t __riscv_vadc_vim_i8m1(vint8m1_t vs2, int8_t imm, vbool8_t carryin, size_t vl);

// vsbc - result in tuple (borrow_out, difference)
vint8m1_t __riscv_vsbc_vvm_i8m1(vint8m1_t vs2, vint8m1_t vs1, vbool8_t borrowin, size_t vl);
vint8m1_t __riscv_vsbc_vxm_i8m1(vint8m1_t vs2, int8_t rs1, vbool8_t borrowin, size_t vl);
```

Note: Intrinsics return only the low SEW result; the carry/borrow-out is returned via a pointer argument in some implementations, or use `vadc.vvm` with `v0` output explicitly. The `vbool8_t` type represents `v0` mask input.

Logical right shift (`vsrl`) performs unsigned zero-fill right shift on each element. The shift amount comes from a vector (`vv`), scalar register (`vx`), or 5-bit immediate (`vi`). Zero bits fill vacated high positions regardless of element sign.

Assembly formats:
```
vsrl.vv vd, vs2, vs1, vm   # vd[i] = vs2[i] >> vs1[i]
vsrl.vx vd, vs2, rs1, vm   # vd[i] = vs2[i] >> x[rs1]
vsrl.vi vd, vs2, uimm5, vm # vd[i] = vs2[i] >> uimm5
```
`vs2` = source data; `vs1`/`rs1`/`uimm5` = shift amount (modulo element width); `vd` = destination; `vm` = mask.

Intrinsics:
```c
// vv: vector shift amount
vuint8mf8_t __riscv_vsrl_vv_u8mf8(vuint8mf8_t vs2, vuint8mf8_t vs1, size_t vl);
vuint16mf4_t __riscv_vsrl_vv_u16mf4(vuint16mf4_t vs2, vuint16mf4_t vs1, size_t vl);
// ... (all EEW/EMUL combinations)

// vx: scalar shift amount
vuint8mf8_t __riscv_vsrl_vx_u8mf8(vuint8mf8_t vs2, size_t rs1, size_t vl);
// ... 

// vi: immediate shift amount (0-31)
vuint8mf8_t __riscv_vsrl_vi_u8mf8(vuint8mf8_t vs2, int32_t uimm5, size_t vl);
// ...
```
Shift amounts ≥ element width yield zero. Intrinsics use unsigned integer types (`vuint*`) reflecting zero-fill semantics.

Narrowing logical right shift instructions `vnsrl.wv`, `vnsrl.wx`, and `vnsrl.wi` shift a double-width source vector right by a shift amount, producing a narrow result. The `.wv` form uses a vector shift amount, `.wx` uses a scalar integer register, and `.wi` uses a 5-bit immediate. The destination element width (SEW) is half the source element width (2*SEW).

Assembly formats:
```
vnsrl.wv vd, vs2, vs1, vm   # vd[i] = vs2[i] >> vs1[i]
vnsrl.wx vd, vs2, rs1, vm   # vd[i] = vs2[i] >> x[rs1]
vnsrl.wi vd, vs2, uimm, vm  # vd[i] = vs2[i] >> uimm
```

Operand order: destination `vd`, double-width source `vs2`, shift amount (`vs1`/`rs1`/`uimm`), with optional mask `vm`.

Intrinsics:
```
// .wv form
vint##m##f##t##_t __riscv_vnsrl_wv_i##m##f##t(vint##m2##f##t##_t vs2, vuint##m##f##t##_t vs1, size_t vl);
vuint##m##f##t##_t __riscv_vnsrl_wv_u##m##f##t(vuint##m2##f##t##_t vs2, vuint##m##f##t##_t vs1, size_t vl);

// .wx form
vint##m##f##t##_t __riscv_vnsrl_wx_i##m##f##t(vint##m2##f##t##_t vs2, size_t rs1, size_t vl);
vuint##m##f##t##_t __riscv_vnsrl_wx_u##m##f##t(vuint##m2##f##t##_t vs2, size_t rs1, size_t vl);

// .wi form
vint##m##f##t##_t __riscv_vnsrl_wi_i##m##f##t(vint##m2##f##t##_t vs2, int32_t uimm, size_t vl);
vuint##m##f##t##_t __riscv_vnsrl_wi_u##m##f##t(vuint##m2##f##t##_t vs2, int32_t uimm, size_t vl);
```

(Replace `##` with LMUL value, `f` with fractional LMUL indicator if needed, `t` with SEW in bits, `m2` indicates double-width source LMUL)

Narrowing arithmetic right shift instructions (`vnsra.wv`, `vnsra.wx`, `vnsra.wi`) shift a double-width signed source vector right by a scalar or vector shift amount, producing a half-width result with sign extension. The `.w` suffix indicates the source is 2×SEW (double-width) while the destination is SEW. The shift amount is treated as unsigned and saturates at the source width.

Assembly formats:
```
vnsra.wv vd, vs2, vs1, vm   # vector shift amount
vnsra.wx vd, vs2, rs1, vm   # scalar x-reg shift amount
vnsra.wi vd, vs2, uimm, vm  # immediate shift amount (0-31)
```
Operand order: `vd` (narrow destination, SEW), `vs2` (wide signed source, 2×SEW), `vs1/rs1/uimm` (shift amount). Note `vs2` is the *first* source operand in assembly.

Intrinsics:
```c
// vector shift amount
vint8m1_t __riscv_vnsra_wv_i8m1(vint16m2_t vs2, vuint8m1_t vs1, size_t vl);
// scalar shift amount (x-reg)
vint8m1_t __riscv_vnsra_wx_i8m1(vint16m2_t vs2, size_t rs1, size_t vl);
// immediate shift amount
vint8m1_t __riscv_vnsra_wi_i8m1(vint16m2_t vs2, int32_t uimm, size_t vl);
```
Examples shown for SEW=8, LMUL=1 (widen to `vint16m2_t` source); patterns apply to other SEW/LMUL combinations with appropriate type suffixes.

`vmslt` (signed) and `vmsltu` (unsigned) compare integer vectors element-wise, producing a mask register result where each bit is set if `vs2[i] < vs1[i]` (`.vv` form, vector-vector) or `vs2[i] < x[rs1]` (`.vx` form, vector-scalar).

**Assembly formats:**
```
vmslt.vv  vd, vs2, vs1, vm   # vd[i] = vs2[i] < vs1[i]  (signed)
vmslt.vx  vd, vs2, rs1, vm   # vd[i] = vs2[i] < x[rs1]  (signed)
vmsltu.vv vd, vs2, vs1, vm   # vd[i] = vs2[i] < vs1[i]  (unsigned)
vmsltu.vx vd, vs2, rs1, vm   # vd[i] = vs2[i] < x[rs1]  (unsigned)
```

`vm` suffix: when present (masked), only set result bits where `v0.mask[i]` is set; unmasked form writes all active elements.

**Intrinsics:**
```c
// Signed
vbool1_t __riscv_vmslt_vv_i8mf8_b64 (vint8mf8_t vs2, vint8mf8_t vs1, size_t vl);
vbool1_t __riscv_vmslt_vx_i8mf8_b64 (vint8mf8_t vs2, int8_t rs1, size_t vl);
// Unsigned
vbool1_t __riscv_vmsltu_vv_u8mf8_b64 (vuint8mf8_t vs2, vuint8mf8_t vs1, size_t vl);
vbool1_t __riscv_vmsltu_vx_u8mf8_b64 (vuint8mf8_t vs2, uint8_t rs1, size_t vl);
```

Masked variants append `_m` (e.g., `__riscv_vmslt_vv_i8mf8_b64_m`).

These instructions compare vector elements and write boolean mask results to a vector mask register (`v0`–`v31`).

**Signed comparisons (`vmsle`)**
- `vmsle.vv vd, vs2, vs1, vm` — vd[i] = vs2[i] ≤ vs1[i]
- `vmsle.vx vd, vs2, rs1, vm` — vd[i] = vs2[i] ≤ x[rs1]
- `vmsle.vi vd, vs2, imm, vm` — vd[i] = vs2[i] ≤ imm (imm: -16 to 15)

**Unsigned comparisons (`vmsleu`)**
- `vmsleu.vv vd, vs2, vs1, vm` — vd[i] = vs2[i] ≤ vs1[i] (unsigned)
- `vmsleu.vx vd, vs2, rs1, vm` — vd[i] = vs2[i] ≤ x[rs1] (unsigned)
- `vmsleu.vi vd, vs2, imm, vm` — vd[i] = vs2[i] ≤ imm (unsigned, imm: 0 to 15)

**Semantics:** `vd` is a mask register; each bit corresponds to one element comparison. The `vm` suffix indicates unmasked (`vmsle.vv`) or masked (`vmsle.vv v0.t`) operation. The first operand (`vs2`) is the left-hand side; the second (`vs1`/`rs1`/`imm`) is the right-hand side.

**Intrinsics:**
```c
vbool1_t __riscv_vmsle_vv_i8m1_b8(vint8m1_t vs2, vint8m1_t vs1, size_t vl);
vbool1_t __riscv_vmsle_vx_i8m1_b8(vint8m1_t vs2, int8_t rs1, size_t vl);
vbool1_t __riscv_vmsle_vi_i8m1_b8(vint8m1_t vs2, int8_t imm, size_t vl);
vbool1_t __riscv_vmsleu_vv_u8m1_b8(vuint8m1_t vs2, vuint8m1_t vs1, size_t vl);
vbool1_t __riscv_vmsleu_vx_u8m1_b8(vuint8m1_t vs2, uint8_t rs1, size_t vl);
vbool1_t __riscv_vmsleu_vi_u8m1_b8(vuint8m1_t vs2, uint8_t imm, size_t vl);
```
(Replace `i8m1`/`u8m1`/`b8` with appropriate type/lmul/mask width; `_m` variants for masked execution.)

`vmul.vv` and `vmul.vx` perform single-width integer multiplication, keeping the **low half** (least-significant bits) of the full product, discarding overflow. The result width equals the operand width (SEW × SEW → SEW).

**Assembly formats:**
```
vmul.vv vd, vs2, vs1, vm   # vd[i] = low(vs2[i] × vs1[i])
vmul.vx vd, vs2, rs1, vm   # vd[i] = low(vs2[i] × x[rs1])
```
- `vd`: destination vector register
- `vs2`: first multiplicand (vector)
- `vs1`/`rs1`: second multiplicand (vector/scalar)
- `vm`: optional mask (t-true, f-hidden)

**Masking:** Unmasked (`vm` omitted) or masked (`v0.t`). Masked elements in `vd` are unchanged (undisturbed) or zeroed (agnostic) per `vtype` setting.

**Intrinsics:**
```c
// vmul.vv
vint8m1_t __riscv_vmul_vv_i8m1(vint8m1_t vs2, vint8m1_t vs1, size_t vl);
vint16m1_t __riscv_vmul_vv_i16m1(vint16m1_t vs2, vint16m1_t vs1, size_t vl);
// ... (i32, i64; u8, u16, u32, u64; other LMULs)

// vmul.vx
vint8m1_t __riscv_vmul_vx_i8m1(vint8m1_t vs2, int8_t rs1, size_t vl);
// ... (analogous signed/unsigned variants)

// Masked variants
vint8m1_t __riscv_vmul_vv_i8m1_m(vbool8_t mask, vint8m1_t vs2, vint8m1_t vs1, size_t vl);
```

`vmulh`, `vmulhu`, and `vmulhsu` return the upper half of a 2×SEW-bit product, discarding the low half. The `.vv` forms multiply two vector operands; `.vx` forms multiply a vector by a scalar `rs1`.

**Signedness and variants:**
- `vmulh.vv/vx`: signed × signed, returns signed high half
- `vmulhu.vv/vx`: unsigned × unsigned, returns unsigned high half  
- `vmulhsu.vv/vx`: signed (first operand) × unsigned (second operand), returns signed high half

**Operand order:** For `.vv`, `vd[i] = high_half(vs2[i] * vs1[i])`. For `.vx`, `vd[i] = high_half(vs2[i] * x[rs1])`. Note `vmulhsu` places the signed value in `vs2` (vector) and unsigned in `vs1`/`rs1`.

**Intrinsics:**
```c
// vmulh: signed × signed
__riscv_vmulh_vv_i8m1(__riscv_vint8m1_t vs2, __riscv_vint8m1_t vs1, size_t vl);
__riscv_vmulh_vx_i8m1(__riscv_vint8m1_t vs2, int8_t rs1, size_t vl);

// vmulhu: unsigned × unsigned
__riscv_vmulhu_vv_u8m1(__riscv_vuint8m1_t vs2, __riscv_vuint8m1_t vs1, size_t vl);
__riscv_vmulhu_vx_u8m1(__riscv_vuint8m1_t vs2, uint8_t rs1, size_t vl);

// vmulhsu: signed × unsigned
__riscv_vmulhsu_vv_i8m1(__riscv_vint8m1_t vs2, __riscv_vuint8m1_t vs1, size_t vl);
__riscv_vmulhsu_vx_i8m1(__riscv_vint8m1_t vs2, uint8_t rs1, size_t vl);
```

Widening integer multiplies produce a destination vector with double the width of the source operands. For `vwmul` (signed) and `vwmulu` (unsigned), SEW_dest = 2×SEW_src. The `.vv` forms multiply two vectors; `.vx` forms multiply a vector by a scalar in `rs1`.

**Assembly formats:**
```
vwmul.vv  vd, vs2, vs1, vm   # vd[i] = vs2[i] × vs1[i]   (signed × signed)
vwmul.vx  vd, vs2, rs1, vm   # vd[i] = vs2[i] × x[rs1]   (signed × signed)
vwmulu.vv vd, vs2, vs1, vm   # vd[i] = vs2[i] × vs1[i]   (unsigned × unsigned)
vwmulu.vx vd, vs2, rs1, vm   # vd[i] = vs2[i] × x[rs1]   (unsigned × unsigned)
```

**Intrinsics:**
```c
// Signed widening multiply
vint16m2_t __riscv_vwmul_vv_i16m2(vint8m1_t vs2, vint8m1_t vs1, size_t vl);
vint16m2_t __riscv_vwmul_vx_i16m2(vint8m1_t vs2, int8_t rs1, size_t vl);

// Unsigned widening multiply
vuint16m2_t __riscv_vwmulu_vv_u16m2(vuint8m1_t vs2, vuint8m1_t vs1, size_t vl);
vuint16m2_t __riscv_vwmulu_vx_u16m2(vuint8m1_t vs2, uint8_t rs1, size_t vl);
```

Operand order: `vs2` is the multiplicand vector, `vs1`/`rs1` is the multiplier. The result width is always 2×SEW of the source operands.

`vnmsac` and `vnmsub` both compute negated multiply-add but differ in which operand is overwritten and the accumulation sign.

**vnmsac.vv/vx** (Vector/Scalar Multiply-Subtract from Accumulator):
- Operation: `vd[i] = -(vs1[i] * vs2[i]) + vd[i]` (vv) or `vd[i] = -(rs1 * vs2[i]) + vd[i]` (vx)
- The multiplicand `vs2` is preserved; `vd` is both addend and destination
- Assembly: `vnmsac.vv vd, vs1, vs2, vm` / `vnmsac.vx vd, rs1, vs2, vm`

**vnmsub.vv/vx** (Vector/Scalar Negate Multiply-Add):
- Operation: `vd[i] = -(vs1[i] * vd[i]) + vs2[i]` (vv) or `vd[i] = -(rs1 * vd[i]) + vs2[i]` (vx)
- The addend `vs2` is preserved; `vd` is multiplicand and destination
- Assembly: `vnmsub.vv vd, vs1, vs2, vm` / `vnmsub.vx vd, rs1, vs2, vm`

**Overwrite distinction**: `vnmsac` overwrites the accumulator `vd` while preserving the multiplicand `vs2`; `vnmsub` overwrites the multiplicand `vd` while preserving the addend `vs2`.

**RVV C Intrinsics**:
```c
// vnmsac: vd = -(vs1 * vs2) + vd
vint32m1_t __riscv_vnmsac_vv_i32m1(vint32m1_t vd, vint32m1_t vs1, vint32m1_t vs2, size_t vl);
vint32m1_t __riscv_vnmsac_vx_i32m1(vint32m1_t vd, int32_t rs1, vint32m1_t vs2, size_t vl);

// vnmsub: vd = -(vs1 * vd) + vs2
vint32m1_t __riscv_vnmsub_vv_i32m1(vint32m1_t vd, vint32m1_t vs1, vint32m1_t vs2, size_t vl);
vint32m1_t __riscv_vnmsub_vx_i32m1(vint32m1_t vd, int32_t rs1, vint32m1_t vs2, size_t vl);
```

Averaging subtraction computes `(src2 - src1) >> 1` with rounding controlled by `vxrm`. `vasub` (signed) and `vasubu` (unsigned) differ only in signedness of the subtraction and shift. The `vxrm` CSR selects rounding: rnu (0), rne (1), rdn (2), rod (3). Operand order: `vd, vs2, vs1` (vv) or `vd, vs2, rs1` (vx), computing `(vs2 - vs1/rs1) >> 1`.

Formats:
```
vasub.vv  vd, vs2, vs1, vm
vasub.vx  vd, vs2, rs1, vm
vasubu.vv vd, vs2, vs1, vm
vasubu.vx vd, vs2, rs1, vm
```

Intrinsics (`vxrm` passed as `vxrm_t` enum):
```
// Signed
__riscv_vasub_vv_i8mf8(vint8mf8_t vs2, vint8mf8_t vs1, vxrm_t vxrm, size_t vl);
__riscv_vasub_vx_i8mf8(vint8mf8_t vs2, int8_t rs1, vxrm_t vxrm, size_t vl);

// Unsigned
__riscv_vasubu_vv_u8mf8(vuint8mf8_t vs2, vuint8mf8_t vs1, vxrm_t vxrm, size_t vl);
__riscv_vasubu_vx_u8mf8(vuint8mf8_t vs2, uint8_t rs1, vxrm_t vxrm, size_t vl);
```
(Available for all standard LMUL/SEW combinations; `vxrm_t` values: `__RISCV_VXRM_RNU`, `__RISCV_VXRM_RNE`, `__RISCV_VXRM_RDN`, `__RISCV_VXRM_ROD`.)

`vssrl` performs a *scaling* logical right shift: it right-shifts elements then rounds the result according to `vxrm`. The shift amount is treated as unsigned. The "scaling" behavior means the result is effectively `(src1 >> (shift_amount & (SEW-1)))` with rounding, useful for fixed-point arithmetic.

**Rounding (`vxrm`):** The `vxrm` CSR (bits [1:0]) controls rounding of the discarded bits: 0=rnu (round to nearest, ties to even), 1=rne (round to nearest, ties to away from zero), 2=rdn (round down/truncate), 3=rod (round to odd). Rounding uses the bits shifted out.

**Shift amount handling:** Only the low `log2(SEW)` bits of the shift amount are used (e.g., 5 bits for SEW=32). Larger values are masked.

**Assembly formats:**
```
vssrl.vv vd, vs2, vs1, vm   # vd[i] = round(vs2[i] >> vs1[i])
vssrl.vx vd, vs2, rs1, vm   # vd[i] = round(vs2[i] >> x[rs1])
vssrl.vi vd, vs2, uimm, vm  # vd[i] = round(vs2[i] >> uimm), uimm in 0..31
```

**Intrinsics:**
```c
// vxrm-controlled rounding; shift from vector, scalar, or immediate
vint8m1_t __riscv_vssrl_vv_i8m1(vint8m1_t vs2, vuint8m1_t vs1, size_t vl);
vint8m1_t __riscv_vssrl_vx_i8m1(vint8m1_t vs2, size_t rs1, size_t vl);
vint8m1_t __riscv_vssrl_vi_i8m1(vint8m1_t vs2, int32_t uimm, size_t vl);
// Unsigned variants: vuint8m1_t __riscv_vssrl_vv_u8m1(...)
// Masked variants: __riscv_vssrl_vv_i8m1_m(vbool8_t vm, ...)
```

Wide-source floating-point addition instructions `vfwadd.wv` and `vfwadd.wf` add a *narrow* source to an already *wide* source (`vs2`), producing a wide result. This differs from normal widening addition where both sources are narrow.

**Operand roles:**
- `vd`: destination (wide, SEW = 2×SEW of narrow source)
- `vs2`: first source, **already widened** (same SEW as `vd`)
- `vs1`/`rs1`/`imm`: second source, **narrow** (SEW of source group, half of `vd`/`vs2`)

**Assembly formats:**
```
vfwadd.wv vd, vs2, vs1, vm   # vector-vector: wide vs2 + narrow vs1
vfwadd.wf vd, vs2, rs1, vm   # vector-scalar: wide vs2 + narrow scalar in rs1
```

**Intrinsics:**
```c
// vfwadd.wv
vfloat32mf2_t __riscv_vfwadd_wv_f32mf2(vfloat32mf2_t vs2, vfloat16mf4_t vs1, size_t vl);
vfloat64m1_t __riscv_vfwadd_wv_f64m1(vfloat64m1_t vs2, vfloat32mf2_t vs1, size_t vl);

// vfwadd.wf
vfloat32mf2_t __riscv_vfwadd_wf_f32mf2(vfloat32mf2_t vs2, float16_t rs1, size_t vl);
vfloat64m1_t __riscv_vfwadd_wf_f64m1(vfloat64m1_t vs2, float32_t rs1, size_t vl);
```

The `.w` suffix denotes "wide source" (vs2 is double-width), while `.v`/`.f` distinguish vector/scalar narrow addends.

`vfwmsac` and `vfwnmsac` are widening floating-point fused multiply-subtract instructions that compute `±(widen(vs1) * widen(vs2)) ± vd` with the wide addend `vd` overwritten by the result. Both use SEW/2 for source operands and SEW for the destination/addend.

**Subtraction/sign behavior:**
- `vfwmsac`: `vd[i] = -(vs1[i] * vs2[i]) + vd[i]`  (negated product, then add)
- `vfwnmsac`: `vd[i] = +(vs1[i] * vs2[i]) - vd[i]`  (positive product, then subtract addend)

**Operand order:**
- `.vv`: `vfwmsac.vv vd, vs2, vs1`  (vd=dest/addend, vs2, vs1=multiplicands)
- `.vf`: `vfwmsac.vf vd, rs1, vs2`  (vd=dest/addend, rs1=scalar multiplicand, vs2=vector multiplicand)

Note: The assembly operand order places the scalar `rs1` second in `.vf` form, with `vs2` as the third operand (vector multiplicand).

**Intrinsics:**
```c
// vfwmsac: vd = -vs1*vs2 + vd
vfloat32m1_t __riscv_vfwmsac_vv_f32m1(vfloat32m1_t vd, vfloat16m1_t vs1, vfloat16m1_t vs2, size_t vl);
vfloat32m1_t __riscv_vfwmsac_vf_f32m1(vfloat32m1_t vd, float16_t rs1, vfloat16m1_t vs2, size_t vl);

// vfwnmsac: vd = +vs1*vs2 - vd
vfloat32m1_t __riscv_vfwnmsac_vv_f32m1(vfloat32m1_t vd, vfloat16m1_t vs1, vfloat16m1_t vs2, size_t vl);
vfloat32m1_t __riscv_vfwnmsac_vf_f32m1(vfloat32m1_t vd, float16_t rs1, vfloat16m1_t vs2, size_t vl);
```

Narrowing double-width integer-to-floating-point conversion instructions convert 2*SEW-width integers in a source vector to SEW-width floating-point values in the destination, with rounding per `frm`. `vfncvt.f.xu.w` converts **unsigned** integers (zero-extended interpretation), while `vfncvt.f.x.w` converts **signed** integers (two's complement interpretation). Both produce single-width FP results half the width of the integer source.

**Assembly format:**
```
vfncvt.f.xu.w vd, vs2, vm   # unsigned integer to FP
vfncvt.f.x.w  vd, vs2, vm   # signed integer to FP
```
- `vd`: destination vector register (SEW-width FP)
- `vs2`: source vector register (2*SEW-width integer)
- `vm`: optional mask (`v0.t` or omitted for unmasked)

**Rounding:** Controlled by `frm` CSR; rounds to nearest even by default. Valid for all IEEE-754 rounding modes.

**RVV C intrinsics:**
```c
// Unsigned source (2*SEW -> SEW FP)
vfloat16mf4_t __riscv_vfncvt_f_xu_w_f16mf4(vuint32mf2_t vs2, size_t vl);
vfloat16mf2_t __riscv_vfncvt_f_xu_w_f16mf2(vuint32m1_t vs2, size_t vl);
vfloat16m1_t  __riscv_vfncvt_f_xu_w_f16m1(vuint32m2_t vs2, size_t vl);
vfloat16m2_t  __riscv_vfncvt_f_xu_w_f16m2(vuint32m4_t vs2, size_t vl);
vfloat16m4_t  __riscv_vfncvt_f_xu_w_f16m4(vuint32m8_t vs2, size_t vl);
vfloat32mf2_t __riscv_vfncvt_f_xu_w_f32mf2(vuint64m1_t vs2, size_t vl);
vfloat32m1_t  __riscv_vfncvt_f_xu_w_f32m1(vuint64m2_t vs2, size_t vl);
vfloat32m2_t  __riscv_vfncvt_f_xu_w_f32m2(vuint64m4_t vs2, size_t vl);
vfloat32m4_t  __riscv_vfncvt_f_xu_w_f32m4(vuint64m8_t vs2, size_t vl);

// Signed source (2*SEW -> SEW FP)
vfloat16mf4_t __riscv_vfncvt_f_x_w_f16mf4(vint32mf2_t vs2, size_t vl);
vfloat16mf2_t __riscv_vfncvt_f_x_w_f16mf2(vint32m1_t vs2, size_t vl);
vfloat16m1_t  __riscv_vfncvt_f_x_w_f16m1(vint32m2_t vs2, size_t vl);
vfloat16m2_t  __riscv_vfncvt_f_x_w_f16m2(vint32m4_t vs2, size_t vl);
vfloat16m4_t  __riscv_vfncvt_f_x_w_f16m4(vint32m8_t vs2, size_t vl);
vfloat32mf2_t __riscv_vfncvt_f_x_w_f32mf2(vint64m1_t vs2, size_t vl);
vfloat32m1_t  __riscv_vfncvt_f_x_w_f32m1(vint64m2_t vs2, size_t vl);
vfloat32m2_t  __riscv_vfncvt_f_x_w_f32m2(vint64m4_t vs2, size_t vl);
vfloat32m4_t  __riscv_vfncvt_f_x_w_f32m4(vint64m8_t vs2, size_t vl);
```
Masked variants append `_m` (destination merge) or `_mu` (destination undisturbed) with `vbool_t mask` as first argument.

Narrowing floating-point to floating-point conversion packs wider elements into narrower ones. `vfncvt.f.f.w` performs ordinary rounding per the dynamic rounding mode in `frm` (defaulting to round-to-nearest, ties-to-even). `vfncvt.rod.f.f.w` always rounds to odd (a specific rounding mode useful for exact error analysis), ignoring `frm`.

Both instructions read double-width source elements from `vs2` and write half-width results to `vd`. The `vm` operand provides mask control. Assembly format:

```
vfncvt.f.f.w vd, vs2, vm
vfncvt.rod.f.f.w vd, vs2, vm
```

Intrinsics (source vector type matches SEW=2×LMUL, destination SEW=LMUL):

```
vfloat16mf4_t __riscv_vfncvt_f_f_w_f16mf4(vfloat32mf2_t vs2, size_t vl);
vfloat16mf4_t __riscv_vfncvt_rod_f_f_w_f16mf4(vfloat32mf2_t vs2, size_t vl);
vfloat16mf2_t __riscv_vfncvt_f_f_w_f16mf2(vfloat32m1_t vs2, size_t vl);
