| Instruction | Assembly Format | Operand Roles |
|-------------|-----------------|---------------|
| `vfsgnjx.vv` | `vfsgnjx.vv vd, vs2, vs1, vm` | `vs2`: magnitude source; `vs1`: sign source; result = magnitude of `vs2` with sign = `sign(vs2) XOR sign(vs1)` |
| `vfsgnjx.vf` | `vfsgnjx.vf vd, vs2, rs1, vm` | `vs2`: magnitude source; `rs1`: scalar sign source; sign = `sign(vs2) XOR sign(rs1)` |

**Semantics:** The instruction takes the **magnitude** (exponent + mantissa) from the first operand (`vs2`) and computes the result's sign bit as the **XOR of the two input sign bits**. This is distinct from arithmetic XOR of full FP values, which would corrupt exponent and mantissa fields.

**Matching RVV C Intrinsics:**
```c
// Vector-vector
vfloat32m1_t __riscv_vfsgnjx_vv_f32m1(vfloat32m1_t vs2, vfloat32m1_t vs1, size_t vl);
vfloat64m1_t __riscv_vfsgnjx_vv_f64m1(vfloat64m1_t vs2, vfloat64m1_t vs1, size_t vl);
// (and LMUL variants: f32m2, f32m4, f32m8, f64m2, f64m4, f64m8)

// Vector-scalar
vfloat32m1_t __riscv_vfsgnjx_vf_f32m1(vfloat32m1_t vs2, float32_t rs1, size_t vl);
vfloat64m1_t __riscv_vfsgnjx_vf_f64m1(vfloat64m1_t vs2, float64_t rs1, size_t vl);
// (and LMUL variants)
```

Operand order in intrinsics: `vs2` (magnitude) first, then `vs1`/`rs1` (sign source), matching assembly.

The claim is **incorrect**. `vmfeq.vv` and `vmfeq.vf` write **mask results**, not floating-point data vectors.

**RVV Instructions:**

| Instruction | Assembly Format | Operands |
|-------------|-----------------|----------|
| `vmfeq.vv` | `vmfeq.vv vd, vs2, vs1, v0.t` | `vd` = mask destination, `vs2` = first FP vector, `vs1` = second FP vector, `v0.t` = optional mask |
| `vmfeq.vf` | `vmfeq.vf vd, vs2, rs1, v0.t` | `vd` = mask destination, `vs2` = FP vector, `rs1` = scalar FP register, `v0.t` = optional mask |

**Semantics:** These perform **floating-point equality comparison**. Element `i` of `vd` is set to 1 if `vs2[i] == vs1[i]` (or `rs1` for `.vf`), else 0. Result is always a **mask register** (boolean vector), never FP data.

**NaN behavior:** Per IEEE 754, comparisons involving NaN are **unordered** and return false. Thus `vmfeq` sets 0 if either operand is NaN. Quiet NaNs do not raise exceptions; signaling NaNs raise the invalid operation flag.

**RVV C Intrinsics:**
```c
// vmfeq.vv
vbool1_t __riscv_vmfeq_vv_f16mf4_b64(vfloat16mf4_t vs2, vfloat16mf4_t vs1, size_t vl);
vbool1_t __riscv_vmfeq_vv_f16mf4_b64_m(vbool64_t mask, vfloat16mf4_t vs2, vfloat16mf4_t vs1, size_t vl);

// vmfeq.vf (scalar operand)
vbool1_t __riscv_vmfeq_vf_f16mf4_b64(vfloat16mf4_t vs2, _Float16 rs1, size_t vl);
vbool1_t __riscv_vmfeq_vf_f16mf4_b64_m(vbool64_t mask, vfloat16mf4_t vs2, _Float16 rs1, size_t vl);
```
(Other EEW/LMUL combinations follow the same pattern: `__riscv_vmfeq_vv_*_b*` and `__riscv_vmfeq_vf_*_b*`.)

The claim is **incorrect**. `vmfle.vv` and `vmfle.vf` compare for **less-than-or-equal**, not greater-than-or-equal.

**Instructions:**

| Instruction | Assembly Format | Semantics |
|-------------|-----------------|-----------|
| `vmfle.vv` | `vmfle.vv vd, vs2, vs1, vm` | `vd[i] = vs2[i] ≤ vs1[i]` |
| `vmfle.vf` | `vmfle.vf vd, vs2, rs1, vm` | `vd[i] = vs2[i] ≤ f[rs1]` |

- **Operand order**: `vs2` is first source (left-hand side), `vs1`/`rs1` is second source (right-hand side); result is `vs2 ≤ vs2`
- **Mask destination**: `vd` is a mask register (single bit per element)
- **NaN behavior**: If either operand is NaN, the comparison is **false** (ordered comparison, signaling)

**Matching C intrinsics:**

```c
// vmfle.vv
vbool1_t __riscv_vmfle_vv_f16mf4_b64(vfloat16mf4_t vs2, vfloat16mf4_t vs1, size_t vl);
vbool1_t __riscv_vmfle_vv_f16mf4_b64_m(vbool64_t vm, vfloat16mf4_t vs2, vfloat16mf4_t vs1, size_t vl);
// ... (similar for other EEW/LMUL: f16mf2, f16m1, f16m2, f16m4, f16m8, f32, f64)

// vmfle.vf
vbool1_t __riscv_vmfle_vf_f16mf4_b64(vfloat16mf4_t vs2, _Float16 rs1, size_t vl);
vbool1_t __riscv_vmfle_vf_f16mf4_b64_m(vbool64_t vm, vfloat16mf4_t vs2, _Float16 rs1, size_t vl);
// ... (similar for other EEW/LMUL)
```

For **greater-than-or-equal**, use `vmfge.vv`/`vmfge.vf` (which are assembler pseudoinstructions for `vmfle.vv`/`vmfle.vf` with swapped operands: `vmfge.vv vd, vs2, vs1` = `vmfle.vv vd, vs1, vs2`).

`vfcvt.xu.f.v` converts floating-point to **unsigned** integer, while `vfcvt.x.f.v` converts to **signed** integer. Both produce same-width results (SEW in, SEW out) and raise the invalid exception for out-of-range values or NaN, producing the largest representable integer (unsigned `2^SEW-1` or signed `2^(SEW-1)-1` respectively).

**Instruction formats:**
```
vfcvt.xu.f.v vd, vs2, vm   # unsigned: float → uint
vfcvt.x.f.v  vd, vs2, vm   # signed:   float → int
```
`vd` = destination vector (integer), `vs2` = source vector (float), `vm` = mask.

**Rounding:** Both use the **dynamic rounding mode** in `frm` (or `rne` if `frm=111`).

**Intrinsics:**
```c
// vfcvt.xu.f.v
vuint8mf8_t  __riscv_vfcvt_xu_f_v_u8mf8 (vfloat8mf8_t vs2, size_t vl);
vuint16mf4_t __riscv_vfcvt_xu_f_v_u16mf4(vfloat16mf4_t vs2, size_t vl);
// ... (all SEW/LMUL combinations)

// vfcvt.x.f.v
vint8mf8_t   __riscv_vfcvt_x_f_v_i8mf8  (vfloat8mf8_t vs2, size_t vl);
vint16mf4_t  __riscv_vfcvt_x_f_v_i16mf4 (vfloat16mf4_t vs2, size_t vl);
// ... (all SEW/LMUL combinations)
```

Both support masked variants: `__riscv_vfcvt_xu_f_v_u8mf8_m`, `__riscv_vfcvt_x_f_v_i8mf8_m`.

The `vfcvt.rtz.*` instructions perform same-width floating-point to integer conversion with round-toward-zero (truncation). There are two forms: `vfcvt.rtz.xu.f.v` (result is unsigned integer) and `vfcvt.rtz.x.f.v` (result is signed integer). Both take a floating-point source vector (vs2) and produce an integer result vector of the same SEW width.

**Instruction formats:**
```
vfcvt.rtz.xu.f.v vd, vs2, vm   # unsigned result
vfcvt.rtz.x.f.v  vd, vs2, vm   # signed result
```

**Intrinsics:**
```c
// Unsigned result
vuint8mf8_t __riscv_vfcvt_rtz_xu_f_v_u8mf8(vfloat8mf8_t vs2, size_t vl);
vuint16mf4_t __riscv_vfcvt_rtz_xu_f_v_u16mf4(vfloat16mf4_t vs2, size_t vl);
vuint32mf2_t __riscv_vfcvt_rtz_xu_f_v_u32mf2(vfloat32mf2_t vs2, size_t vl);
vuint64m1_t __riscv_vfcvt_rtz_xu_f_v_u64m1(vfloat64m1_t vs2, size_t vl);
// ... plus all LMUL variants (mf8..m8)

// Signed result
vint8mf8_t __riscv_vfcvt_rtz_x_f_v_i8mf8(vfloat8mf8_t vs2, size_t vl);
vint16mf4_t __riscv_vfcvt_rtz_x_f_v_i16mf4(vfloat16mf4_t vs2, size_t vl);
vint32mf2_t __riscv_vfcvt_rtz_x_f_v_i32mf2(vfloat32mf2_t vs2, size_t vl);
vint64m1_t __riscv_vfcvt_rtz_x_f_v_i64m1(vfloat64m1_t vs2, size_t vl);
// ... plus all LMUL variants (mf8..m8)
```

The `x`/`xu` in the intrinsic names indicate signed/unsigned integer results respectively; `f` indicates float input; `v` indicates vector operation. The `vm` mask operand is omitted in the intrinsics when using the default `vsetvli`-controlled mask.

`vfcvt.f.xu.v` (unsigned) and `vfcvt.f.x.v` (signed) convert same-width integers to floating-point values. Both use the OPFVV format with vd destination (floating-point), vs2 source (integer), and vm mask. The SEW determines both integer source width and floating-point result width (e.g., SEW=32: 32-bit integer → 32-bit float).

**Formats:**
```
vfcvt.f.xu.v vd, vs2, vm   # unsigned integer to float
vfcvt.f.x.v  vd, vs2, vm   # signed integer to float
```

**Rounding:** Uses the dynamic rounding mode in `frm` (RNE, RTZ, RDN, RUP, RMM). Exact conversions are common; inexact results round per `frm`.

**Intrinsics:**
```c
// unsigned source
vfloat32mf2_t __riscv_vfcvt_f_xu_v_f32mf2(vuint32mf2_t vs2, size_t vl);
vfloat32m1_t  __riscv_vfcvt_f_xu_v_f32m1 (vuint32m1_t  vs2, size_t vl);
// ... (m2, m4, m8, f64 variants)

// signed source
vfloat32mf2_t __riscv_vfcvt_f_x_v_f32mf2(vint32mf2_t vs2, size_t vl);
vfloat32m1_t  __riscv_vfcvt_f_x_v_f32m1 (vint32m1_t  vs2, size_t vl);
// ... (m2, m4, m8, f64 variants)
```

Masked variants add `_m` suffix with `vbool_t` mask argument; masked-off elements preserved.

Widening float-to-integer conversions extend SEW-bit floating-point elements to 2*SEW-bit integers. `vfwcvt.xu.f.v` converts to unsigned, `vfwcvt.x.f.v` to signed. The destination vector register group has EEW=2*SEW and EMUL=2*LMUL.

Assembly formats:
```
vfwcvt.xu.f.v vd, vs2, vm   # vd: unsigned 2*SEW, vs2: float SEW
vfwcvt.x.f.v  vd, vs2, vm   # vd: signed 2*SEW, vs2: float SEW
```
`vd` is the destination vector register group (widen), `vs2` is the source float vector, `vm` is the mask register (v0.t or omitted).

RVV C intrinsics:
```c
// SEW=16, LMUL=1: float16_t -> uint32_t / int32_t
vuint32m2_t __riscv_vfwcvt_xu_f_v_u32m2(vfloat16m1_t vs2, size_t vl);
vint32m2_t  __riscv_vfwcvt_x_f_v_i32m2(vfloat16m1_t vs2, size_t vl);

// SEW=32, LMUL=1: float32_t -> uint64_t / int64_t
vuint64m2_t __riscv_vfwcvt_xu_f_v_u64m2(vfloat32m1_t vs2, size_t vl);
vint64m2_t  __riscv_vfwcvt_x_f_v_i64m2(vfloat32m1_t vs2, size_t vl);
```
Out-of-range values saturate: unsigned saturates to 0 or UINT_MAX, signed to INT_MIN or INT_MAX. NaN converts to 0.

`vfwcvt.f.xu.v` and `vfwcvt.f.x.v` are widening floating-point conversions that produce double-width floating-point results from integer sources. Both use the OPFVV format with `vd` as the destination vector register, `vs2` as the source integer vector, and `vm` for masking.

**Source interpretation:** `vfwcvt.f.xu.v` treats `vs2` as **unsigned** integers; `vfwcvt.f.x.v` treats `vs2` as **signed** integers. The SEW of `vs2` determines the source integer width, and the destination SEW (2×SEW) determines the resulting floating-point format: SEW=8→16 produces `f16`, SEW=16→32 produces `f32`, SEW=32→64 produces `f64`.

**Assembly formats:**
```
vfwcvt.f.xu.v vd, vs2, vm   # unsigned integer to double-width float
vfwcvt.f.x.v  vd, vs2, vm   # signed integer to double-width float
```

**RVV C intrinsics:**
```c
vfloat16mf4_t __riscv_vfwcvt_f_xu_v_f16mf4(vuint8mf8_t vs2, size_t vl);
vfloat16mf2_t __riscv_vfwcvt_f_xu_v_f16mf2(vuint8mf4_t vs2, size_t vl);
vfloat16m1_t  __riscv_vfwcvt_f_xu_v_f16m1 (vuint8mf2_t vs2, size_t vl);
vfloat16m2_t  __riscv_vfwcvt_f_xu_v_f16m2 (vuint8m1_t  vs2, size_t vl);
vfloat16m4_t  __riscv_vfwcvt_f_xu_v_f16m4 (vuint8m2_t  vs2, size_t vl);
vfloat16m8_t  __riscv_vfwcvt_f_xu_v_f16m8 (vuint8m4_t  vs2, size_t vl);

vfloat32mf2_t __riscv_vfwcvt_f_xu_v_f32mf2(vuint16mf4_t vs2, size_t vl);
vfloat32m1_t  __riscv_vfwcvt_f_xu_v_f32m1 (vuint16mf2_t vs2, size_t vl);
vfloat32m2_t  __riscv_vfwcvt_f_xu_v_f32m2 (vuint16m1_t  vs2, size_t vl);
vfloat32m4_t  __riscv_vfwcvt_f_xu_v_f32m4 (vuint16m2_t  vs2, size_t vl);
vfloat32m8_t  __riscv_vfwcvt_f_xu_v_f32m8 (vuint16m4_t  vs2, size_t vl);

vfloat64m1_t __riscv_vfwcvt_f_xu_v_f64m1(vuint32mf2_t vs2, size_t vl);
vfloat64m2_t __riscv_vfwcvt_f_xu_v_f64m2(vuint32m1_t  vs2, size_t vl);
vfloat64m4_t __riscv_vfwcvt_f_xu_v_f64m4(vuint32m2_t  vs2, size_t vl);
vfloat64m8_t __riscv_vfwcvt_f_xu_v_f64m8(vuint32m4_t  vs2, size_t vl);
```

Signed variants replace `xu` with `x` and `vuint` with `vint` (e.g., `__riscv_vfwcvt_f_x_v_f32m1(vint16m1_t vs2, size_t vl)`). Masked variants add `_m` suffix with `vbool_t` mask argument before `vl`.

Both `vfncvt.f.f.w` and `vfncvt.rod.f.f.w` are narrowing floating-point-to-floating-point conversions (2*SEW→SEW). The key difference is rounding: `vfncvt.f.f.w` uses the current dynamic rounding mode, while `vfncvt.rod.f.f.w` always rounds toward odd (ROD), a specific rounding mode useful for avoiding bias in certain numerical algorithms.

**Instruction formats:**
```
vfncvt.f.f.w vd, vs2, vm      # narrowing FP convert, dynamic rounding
vfncvt.rod.f.f.w vd, vs2, vm  # narrowing FP convert, round-to-odd
```

**Narrowing relationship:** Both convert from wider source (2*SEW bits) in `vs2` to narrower destination (SEW bits) in `vd`. For example, SEW=32 means f64→f32 conversion.

**Operand roles:** `vd` = destination vector (narrower result), `vs2` = source vector (wider input), `vm` = optional vector mask (v0.t or omitted).

**Intrinsics:**
```c
// vfncvt.f.f.w - dynamic rounding mode
vfloat16mf4_t __riscv_vfncvt_f_f_w_f16mf4(vfloat32mf2_t vs2, size_t vl);
vfloat32mf2_t __riscv_vfncvt_f_f_w_f32mf2(vfloat64m1_t vs2, size_t vl);

// vfncvt.rod.f.f.w - round-to-odd
vfloat16mf4_t __riscv_vfncvt_rod_f_f_w_f16mf4(vfloat32mf2_t vs2, size_t vl);
vfloat32mf2_t __riscv_vfncvt_rod_f_f_w_f32mf2(vfloat64m1_t vs2, size_t vl);
```

`vredmax.vs` and `vredmaxu.vs` are **reduction** (not element-wise) instructions that compute the maximum across all active elements of a vector, combining with a scalar start value.

**Signed vs. unsigned:** `vredmax.vs` treats elements as signed integers; `vredmaxu.vs` treats them as unsigned.

**Result placement:** The final maximum is written to element 0 of the destination vector register; other elements are written with `1`s (tail-agnostic) or undisturbed (tail-undisturbed) per `vtype` setting.

**Instruction names:** `vredmax.vs` (signed), `vredmaxu.vs` (unsigned).

**Assembly format:**
```
vredmax.vs vd, vs2, vs1, vm   # vd[0] = max(vs1[0], max_active(vs2[*]))
vredmaxu.vs vd, vs2, vs1, vm  # unsigned variant
```
- `vs2`: source vector (elements to reduce)
- `vs1`: scalar start value (element 0)
- `vd`: destination (result in element 0)
- `vm`: optional mask (`v0.t` or omitted for unmasked)

**Intrinsics:**
```c
// Signed
vint8m1_t __riscv_vredmax_vs_i8m8_i8m1(vint8m8_t vector, vint8m1_t scalar, size_t vl);
vint16m1_t __riscv_vredmax_vs_i16m8_i16m1(vint16m8_t vector, vint16m1_t scalar, size_t vl);
// ... (LMUL variants: m1,m2,m4,m8 → m1)

// Unsigned
vuint8m1_t __riscv_vredmaxu_vs_u8m8_u8m1(vuint8m8_t vector, vuint8m1_t scalar, size_t vl);
vuint16m1_t __riscv_vredmaxu_vs_u16m8_u16m1(vuint16m8_t vector, vuint16m1_t scalar, size_t vl);
// ... (LMUL variants)
```

The description is incorrect: `vredmin.vs` and `vredminu.vs` are **reduction** instructions, not element-wise operations. They compute a single scalar minimum across all active elements of a vector, accumulating into a scalar destination.

**Signed vs. Unsigned Interpretation**
- `vredmin.vs`: signed integer minimum reduction (treats elements as two's complement)
- `vredminu.vs`: unsigned integer minimum reduction

**Instruction Formats**

```
vredmin.vs  vd, vs2, vs1, vm   # vd[0] = min(vs1[0], min(vs2[*]))
vredminu.vs vd, vs2, vs1, vm   # vd[0] = minu(vs1[0], min(vs2[*]))
```

**Operand Roles**
- `vd`: scalar destination (element 0), also holds initial value
- `vs2`: vector source (elements to reduce)
- `vs1`: scalar source (element 0 provides initial value)
- `vm`: mask (v0.t or omitted for unmasked)

**Intrinsics**

```c
// Signed
vint8m1_t __riscv_vredmin_vs_i8m1_i8m1(vint8m1_t vector, vint8m1_t scalar, size_t vl);
vint16m1_t __riscv_vredmin_vs_i16m2_i16m1(vint16m2_t vector, vint16m1_t scalar, size_t vl);
// ... (LMUL variants: vs2 LMUL = 1,2,4,8,16; vd/vs1 LMUL = 1)

// Unsigned
vuint8m1_t __riscv_vredminu_vs_u8m1_u8m1(vuint8m1_t vector, vuint8m1_t scalar, size_t vl);
vuint16m1_t __riscv_vredminu_vs_u16m2_u16m1(vuint16m2_t vector, vuint16m1_t scalar, size_t vl);
// ... (same LMUL pattern)
```

The reduction uses `vs1[0]` as the identity/initial value, computes the minimum across all active `vs2` elements, and writes the result to `vd[0]`.

Widening integer reductions `vwredsum.vs` and `vwredsumu.vs` sum vector elements into a double-width accumulator. `vwredsum.vs` treats source elements as signed, sign-extending to 2*SEW; `vwredsumu.vs` treats them as unsigned, zero-extending to 2*SEW. The wide accumulator (vs1[0]) and result are 2*SEW bits, placed in element 0 of the destination vector register; other elements are written with undefined values (typically tail-agnostic policy). SEW for source elements is encoded in `vtype`, with LMUL scaling accordingly.

**Assembly formats:**
```
vwredsum.vs  vd, vs2, vs1, vm  # signed
vwredsumu.vs vd, vs2, vs1, vm  # unsigned
```
- `vs2`: vector of SEW-bit elements to sum
- `vs1[0]`: scalar 2*SEW-bit accumulator (element 0)
- `vd[0]`: 2*SEW-bit result (element 0)
- `vm`: optional mask (v0.t or omitted for unmasked)

**RVV C intrinsics:**
```c
// Signed widening reduction
__riscv_vwredsum_vs_i8m1_i16m1(vint8m1_t vector, vint16m1_t scalar, size_t vl);
__riscv_vwredsum_vs_i16m1_i32m1(vint16m1_t vector, vint32m1_t scalar, size_t vl);
__riscv_vwredsum_vs_i32m1_i64m1(vint32m1_t vector, vint64m1_t scalar, size_t vl);

// Unsigned widening reduction  
__riscv_vwredsumu_vs_u8m1_u16m1(vuint8m1_t vector, vuint16m1_t scalar, size_t vl);
__riscv_vwredsumu_vs_u16m1_u32m1(vuint16m1_t vector, vuint32m1_t scalar, size_t vl);
__riscv_vwredsumu_vs_u32m1_u64m1(vuint32m1_t vector, vuint64m1_t scalar, size_t vl);
```
Masked variants append `_m` (e.g., `__riscv_vwredsum_vs_i8m1_i16m1_m`).

Mask-register logical operations operate on mask registers, not integer vector elements—each bit in a mask register corresponds to one vector element. The instructions perform bitwise logical operations between two mask registers, with results written to a mask register.

**Instruction formats (assembly operand order: vd, vs2, vs1):**
```
vmand.mm  vd, vs2, vs1   # vd = vs2 & vs1
vmnand.mm vd, vs2, vs1   # vd = ~(vs2 & vs1)
vmandn.mm vd, vs2, vs1   # vd = vs2 & ~vs1
vmor.mm   vd, vs2, vs1   # vd = vs2 | vs1
vmnor.mm  vd, vs2, vs1   # vd = ~(vs2 | vs1)
vmorn.mm  vd, vs2, vs1   # vd = vs2 | ~vs1
vmxor.mm  vd, vs2, vs1   # vd = vs2 ^ vs1
vmxnor.mm vd, vs2, vs1   # vd = ~(vs2 ^ vs1)
```

**Intrinsics:**
```
__riscv_vmand_mm_b8(vbool8_t vs2, vbool8_t vs1, size_t vl);
__riscv_vmnand_mm_b8(vbool8_t vs2, vbool8_t vs1, size_t vl);
__riscv_vmandn_mm_b8(vbool8_t vs2, vbool8_t vs1, size_t vl);
__riscv_vmor_mm_b8(vbool8_t vs2, vbool8_t vs1, size_t vl);
__riscv_vmnor_mm_b8(vbool8_t vs2, vbool8_t vs1, size_t vl);
__riscv_vmorn_mm_b8(vbool8_t vs2, vbool8_t vs1, size_t vl);
__riscv_vmxor_mm_b8(vbool8_t vs2, vbool8_t vs1, size_t vl);
__riscv_vmxnor_mm_b8(vbool8_t vs2, vbool8_t vs1, size_t vl);
```
(Also available for `b16`, `b32`, `b64` mask types.)

`vmand.mm` performs a bitwise AND of two mask registers: `vd = vs2 & vs1`. `vmandn.mm` performs AND-with-inverted-second-source: `vd = vs2 & ~vs1`. The "n" suffix indicates the second source (`vs1`) is logically negated before the AND.

**Instruction formats:**
```
vmand.mm  vd, vs2, vs1   # vd[i] = vs2[i] & vs1[i]
vmandn.mm vd, vs2, vs1   # vd[i] = vs2[i] & ~vs1[i]
```
Operand order: `vd` (destination mask), `vs2` (first source mask), `vs1` (second source mask, inverted for `vmandn.mm`). All are mask registers (`v0`–`v31`). The result is written to `vd` under the current masking scheme (unmasked, `v0.t`, or `v0.f`); there is no tail-agnostic vs tail-undisturbed distinction for mask registers.

