vint32m2_t __riscv_vwmacc_vv_i32m2(vint32m2_t vd, vint16m1_t vs1, vint16m1_t vs2, size_t vl);
vint32m2_t __riscv_vwmacc_vx_i32m2(vint32m2_t vd, int16_t rs1, vint16m1_t vs2, size_t vl);

// vwmaccu.vv / vwmaccu.vx
vuint32m2_t __riscv_vwmaccu_vv_u32m2(vuint32m2_t vd, vuint16m1_t vs1, vuint16m1_t vs2, size_t vl);
vuint32m2_t __riscv_vwmaccu_vx_u32m2(vuint32m2_t vd, uint16_t rs1, vuint16m1_t vs2, size_t vl);

// vwmaccsu.vv / vwmaccsu.vx
vint32m2_t __riscv_vwmaccsu_vv_i32m2(vint32m2_t vd, vint16m1_t vs1, vuint16m1_t vs2, size_t vl);
vint32m2_t __riscv_vwmaccsu_vx_i32m2(vint32m2_t vd, int16_t rs1, vuint16m1_t vs2, size_t vl);

// vwmaccus.vx (only .vx form exists)
vint32m2_t __riscv_vwmaccus_vx_i32m2(vint32m2_t vd, uint16_t rs1, vint16m1_t vs2, size_t vl);
```

Note: There is **no `vwmaccus.vv`**; the unsigned-signed combination only exists with scalar unsigned first operand. The `vwmacc` family **overwrites `vd`** with the accumulated result (not in-place multiplicand overwrite—`vd` is the accumulator, `vs2` is the multiplicand that gets read at SEW and widened).

The `vfsgnj` family performs floating-point sign manipulation: `vfsgnj` (sign injection), `vfsgnjn` (sign injection negated), and `vfsgnjx` (sign injection XOR). The `.vv` variants take two vector operands, while `.vf` variants take one vector and one scalar (5-bit immediate encoded as `rs1` field, or from `f[rs1]` in standard interpretation).

**Assembly formats:**
```
vfsgnj.vv  vd, vs2, vs1, vm   # vd[i] = abs(vs2[i]) with sign of vs1[i]
vfsgnj.vf  vd, vs2, rs1, vm   # vd[i] = abs(vs2[i]) with sign of f[rs1]
vfsgnjn.vv vd, vs2, vs1, vm   # vd[i] = abs(vs2[i]) with sign of -vs1[i]
vfsgnjn.vf vd, vs2, rs1, vm   # vd[i] = abs(vs2[i]) with sign of -f[rs1]
vfsgnjx.vv vd, vs2, vs1, vm   # vd[i] = vs2[i] with sign XOR vs1[i]
vfsgnjx.vf vd, vs2, rs1, vm   # vd[i] = vs2[i] with sign XOR f[rs1]
```

**Key distinctions:** The `.vv` forms use per-element signs from vector `vs1`, enabling element-wise operations like `copysign(vs2, vs1)`. The `.vf` forms broadcast a single scalar sign, used for operations like `copysign(v, scalar)`. These are semantically distinct: `.vv` produces N results from 2N inputs; `.vf` produces N results from N+1 inputs with scalar broadcast.

**Intrinsics:**
```c
// vfsgnj.vv / vfsgnj.vf
vfloat32m1_t __riscv_vfsgnj_vv_f32m1(vfloat32m1_t vs2, vfloat32m1_t vs1, size_t vl);
vfloat32m1_t __riscv_vfsgnj_vf_f32m1(vfloat32m1_t vs2, float32_t rs1, size_t vl);

// vfsgnjn.vv / vfsgnjn.vf
vfloat32m1_t __riscv_vfsgnjn_vv_f32m1(vfloat32m1_t vs2, vfloat32m1_t vs1, size_t vl);
vfloat32m1_t __riscv_vfsgnjn_vf_f32m1(vfloat32m1_t vs2, float32_t rs1, size_t vl);

// vfsgnjx.vv / vfsgnjx.vf
vfloat32m1_t __riscv_vfsgnjx_vv_f32m1(vfloat32m1_t vs2, vfloat32m1_t vs1, size_t vl);
vfloat32m1_t __riscv_vfsgnjx_vf_f32m1(vfloat32m1_t vs2, float32_t rs1, size_t vl);
```
(Other SEW/LMUL combinations follow same pattern: `f64m2`, `f16m4`, etc.)

**Non-interchangeability:** Using `.vf` where `.vv` is needed loses per-element sign variation; using `.vv` where `.vf` suffices wastes register file and instruction encoding. The scalar operand in `.vf` is read once, not per-element, affecting performance on microarchitectures with scalar/vector operand divergence. No floating-point exceptions are raised; these are bitwise sign operations with exact results, no rounding.

This describes the **vaadd** (vector averaging add) and **vasub** (vector averaging subtract) instruction families.

**RVV Instructions:**

| Instruction | Assembly Format | Behavior |
|-------------|-----------------|----------|
| vaadd.vv | `vaadd.vv vd, vs2, vs1, vm` | Signed averaging add: (vs2 + vs1 + round) >> 1 |
| vaadd.vx | `vaadd.vx vd, vs2, rs1, vm` | Signed averaging add with scalar |
| vasub.vv | `vasub.vv vd, vs2, vs1, vm` | Signed averaging subtract: (vs2 - vs1 + round) >> 1 |
| vasub.vx | `vasub.vx vd, vs2, rs1, vm` | Signed averaging subtract with scalar |
| vaaddu.vv | `vaaddu.vv vd, vs2, vs1, vm` | Unsigned averaging add |
| vaaddu.vx | `vaaddu.vx vd, vs2, rs1, vm` | Unsigned averaging add with scalar |
| vasubu.vv | `vasubu.vv vd, vs2, vs1, vm` | Unsigned averaging subtract |
| vasubu.vx | `vasubu.vx vd, vs2, rs1, vm` | Unsigned averaging subtract with scalar |

**Key properties:** No saturation (results fit in width); `vxrm` controls rounding (rnu, rne, rdn, rod); `vxsat` unaffected.

**RVV C Intrinsics:**

```c
// Signed
vint8m1_t __riscv_vaadd_vv_i8m1(vint8m1_t vs2, vint8m1_t vs1, size_t vl);
vint8m1_t __riscv_vaadd_vx_i8m1(vint8m1_t vs2, int8_t rs1, size_t vl);
vint8m1_t __riscv_vasub_vv_i8m1(vint8m1_t vs2, vint8m1_t vs1, size_t vl);
vint8m1_t __riscv_vasub_vx_i8m1(vint8m1_t vs2, int8_t rs1, size_t vl);

// Unsigned
vuint8m1_t __riscv_vaaddu_vv_u8m1(vuint8m1_t vs2, vuint8m1_t vs1, size_t vl);
vuint8m1_t __riscv_vaaddu_vx_u8m1(vuint8m1_t vs2, uint8_t rs1, size_t vl);
vuint8m1_t __riscv_vasubu_vv_u8m1(vuint8m1_t vs2, vuint8m1_t vs1, size_t vl);
vuint8m1_t __riscv_vasubu_vx_u8m1(vuint8m1_t vs2, uint8_t rs1, size_t vl);
```

(Masked variants: `__riscv_vaadd_vv_i8m1_m(vbool8_t vm, ...)` etc.; rounding mode argument `vxrm` passed via `__riscv_vsetvli` or explicit `vxrm` parameter in some intrinsics.)

The instruction family is **vmerge**, which performs masked selection between two vector sources.

**RVV Instructions:**

| Instruction | Assembly Format | Description |
|-------------|-----------------|-------------|
| `vmerge.vvm` | `vmerge.vvm vd, vs2, vs1, v0` | vector-vector merge: `v0[i].LSB ? vs1[i] : vs2[i]` |
| `vmerge.vxm` | `vmerge.vxm vd, vs2, rs1, v0` | vector-scalar (x-reg) merge: `v0[i].LSB ? x[rs1] : vs2[i]` |
| `vmerge.vim` | `vmerge.vim vd, vs2, imm, v0` | vector-immediate merge: `v0[i].LSB ? imm : vs2[i]` |

**Operand roles:** `vd` destination, `vs2` = unmasked element source (pass-through when mask bit is 0), `vs1/rs1/imm` = masked element source (selected when mask bit is 1), `v0` = mask register (only LSB used per element).

**C Intrinsics:**
```c
// Vector-vector
vint8m1_t __riscv_vmerge_vvm_i8m1(vint8m1_t vs2, vint8m1_t vs1, vbool8_t mask, size_t vl);
// Vector-scalar (x-reg)
vint8m1_t __riscv_vmerge_vxm_i8m1(vint8m1_t vs2, int8_t rs1, vbool8_t mask, size_t vl);
// Vector-immediate
vint8m1_t __riscv_vmerge_vim_i8m1(vint8m1_t vs2, int8_t imm, vbool8_t mask, size_t vl);
```
(Replace `i8m1`/`vbool8_t` with appropriate type suffixes; intrinsics take `mask` explicitly, not hardcoded `v0`.)

The instruction family is **vssrl** (vector single-width scaling shift right logical for fixed-point rounding).

**RVV Instructions:**

| Instruction | Assembly Format | Semantics |
|-------------|-----------------|-----------|
| vssrl.vv | `vssrl.vv vd, vs2, vs1, vm` | Vector-vector: `vd[i] = roundoff_unsigned(vs2[i], vs1[i][4:0])` |
| vssrl.vx | `vssrl.vx vd, vs2, rs1, vm` | Vector-scalar: `vd[i] = roundoff_unsigned(vs2[i], x[rs1][4:0])` |
| vssrl.vi | `vssrl.vi vd, vs2, uimm, vm` | Vector-immediate: `vd[i] = roundoff_unsigned(vs2[i], uimm)` |

Where `roundoff_unsigned(v, d) = (v >> d) + ((v >> (d-1)) & 1)` for d > 0, and `v` for d = 0. The shift amount is limited to log2(SEW) bits (5 bits for SEW=32).

**RVV C Intrinsics:**

```c
// vssrl.vv
vuint8mf8_t __riscv_vssrl_vv_u8mf8(vuint8mf8_t vs2, vuint8mf8_t vs1, size_t vl);
vuint16mf4_t __riscv_vssrl_vv_u16mf4(vuint16mf4_t vs2, vuint16mf4_t vs1, size_t vl);
vuint32mf2_t __riscv_vssrl_vv_u32mf2(vuint32mf2_t vs2, vuint32mf2_t vs1, size_t vl);
vuint64m1_t __riscv_vssrl_vv_u64m1(vuint64m1_t vs2, vuint64m1_t vs1, size_t vl);
// ... plus all LMUL variants (mf4, mf2, m1, m2, m4, m8)

// vssrl.vx
vuint8mf8_t __riscv_vssrl_vx_u8mf8(vuint8mf8_t vs2, size_t rs1, size_t vl);
// ... (typed by SEW/LMUL, rs1 is unsigned scalar shift amount)

// vssrl.vi
vuint8mf8_t __riscv_vssrl_vi_u8mf8(vuint8mf8_t vs2, uint8_t uimm, size_t vl);
// ... (uimm: 0-31)
```

**Key constraints:** Only unsigned integer types; no masking intrinsics use `vbool_t` mask argument (e.g., `__riscv_vssrl_vv_u8mf8_m` for masked).

The clue points to the **VFDIV** family (vector floating-point divide).

**Instructions and assembly formats:**

| Instruction | Assembly format | Operands |
|-------------|---------------|----------|
| vfdiv.vv | `vfdiv.vv vd, vs2, vs1, vm` | vd=result, vs2=dividend (vector), vs1=divisor (vector) |
| vfdiv.vf | `vfdiv.vf vd, vs2, rs1, vm` | vd=result, vs2=dividend (vector), rs1=divisor (scalar) |
| vfrdiv.vf | `vfrdiv.vf vd, vs2, rs1, vm` | vd=result, vs2=divisor (vector), rs1=dividend (scalar) — **reverse divide** |

**Behavior:** Single-width FP division (SEW = {16,32,64,128} for supported types). Uses dynamic rounding mode in `fcsr`. Raises FP exceptions (invalid, divide-by-zero, overflow, underflow, inexact). Not an estimate—exact IEEE 754 division.

**C intrinsics:**
```c
// vd = vs2 / vs1
vfloat32m1_t __riscv_vfdiv_vv_f32m1(vfloat32m1_t vs2, vfloat32m1_t vs1, size_t vl);
vfloat64m1_t __riscv_vfdiv_vv_f64m1(vfloat64m1_t vs2, vfloat64m1_t vs1, size_t vl);

// vd = vs2 / rs1 (scalar)
vfloat32m1_t __riscv_vfdiv_vf_f32m1(vfloat32m1_t vs2, float rs1, size_t vl);
vfloat64m1_t __riscv_vfdiv_vf_f64m1(vfloat64m1_t vs2, double rs1, size_t vl);

// vd = rs1 / vs2 (reverse)
vfloat32m1_t __riscv_vfrdiv_vf_f32m1(vfloat32m1_t vs2, float rs1, size_t vl);
vfloat64m1_t __riscv_vfrdiv_vf_f64m1(vfloat64m1_t vs2, double rs1, size_t vl);
```

(Replace `m1` with `m2/m4/m8/mf2/mf4` for other LMUL; `f16` variants exist for Zvfh/Zvfhmin.)

The description matches the **vfma** (fused multiply-add, overwrite addend) family.

## RVV Instructions

| Instruction | Assembly Format | Operation |
|-------------|-----------------|-----------|
| `vfma.vv` | `vfma.vv vd, vs1, vs2, vm` | vd[i] = +(vs1[i] × vs2[i]) + vd[i] |
| `vfma.vf` | `vfma.vf vd, rs1, vs2, vm` | vd[i] = +(rs1 × vs2[i]) + vd[i] |

Operand roles: `vd` = accumulator (addend, overwritten), `vs1`/`rs1` = multiplicand, `vs2` = multiplier.

## Negated Variants (fused multiply-sub, overwrite addend)

| Instruction | Assembly Format | Operation |
|-------------|-----------------|-----------|
| `vfms.vv` | `vfms.vv vd, vs1, vs2, vm` | vd[i] = -(vs1[i] × vs2[i]) + vd[i] |
| `vfms.vf` | `vfms.vf vd, rs1, vs2, vm` | vd[i] = -(rs1 × vs2[i]) + vd[i] |

## RVV C Intrinsics

```c
// vfma.vv
vfloat16m1_t __riscv_vfma_vv_f16m1(vfloat16m1_t vd, vfloat16m1_t vs1, vfloat16m1_t vs2, size_t vl);
vfloat32m1_t __riscv_vfma_vv_f32m1(vfloat32m1_t vd, vfloat32m1_t vs1, vfloat32m1_t vs2, size_t vl);
vfloat64m1_t __riscv_vfma_vv_f64m1(vfloat64m1_t vd, vfloat64m1_t vs1, vfloat64m1_t vs2, size_t vl);

// vfma.vf
vfloat16m1_t __riscv_vfma_vf_f16m1(vfloat16m1_t vd, _Float16 rs1, vfloat16m1_t vs2, size_t vl);
vfloat32m1_t __riscv_vfma_vf_f32m1(vfloat32m1_t vd, float rs1, vfloat32m1_t vs2, size_t vl);
vfloat64m1_t __riscv_vfma_vf_f64m1(vfloat64m1_t vd, double rs1, vfloat64m1_t vs2, size_t vl);

// vfms.vv
vfloat16m1_t __riscv_vfms_vv_f16m1(vfloat16m1_t vd, vfloat16m1_t vs1, vfloat16m1_t vs2, size_t vl);
// ... (analogous for f32/f64)

// vfms.vf
vfloat16m1_t __riscv_vfms_vf_f16m1(vfloat16m1_t vd, _Float16 rs1, vfloat16m1_t vs2, size_t vl);
// ... (analogous for f32/f64)
```

Note: `vfma`/`vfms` are **single-width** and **overwrite the addend** (`vd`). For the alternative family that overwrites the multiplicand, see `vfmacc`/`vfmsac`. No widening variants exist for this specific family; widening fused multiply-add uses `vfwmacc`/`vfwmsac` (overwrite multiplicand) or `vfwmaccbf16` (bfloat16).

The square root operation is provided by the **VFSQRT** instruction family in RVV.

**Instruction:** `vfsqrt.v vd, vs2, vm`

**Assembly format:**
```
vfsqrt.v vD, vS2, v0.t   # masked (vm=0)
vfsqrt.v vD, vS2         # unmasked (vm=1)
```

**Operand roles:**
- `vd` (vD): destination vector register, FP result
- `vs2` (vS2): source vector register, FP input elements
- `vm`: vector mask register `v0` (implicit) when `v0.t` present; unmasked when omitted

**Behavior:** Computes IEEE 754 square root of each active SEW-width floating-point element. Uses dynamic rounding mode in `frm`. Sets invalid operation exception for negative inputs (except -0 → -0). Not an estimate—exactly rounded result.

**Intrinsics:**
```c
// Unmasked
vfloat16m1_t __riscv_vfsqrt_v_f16m1(vfloat16m1_t vs2, size_t vl);
vfloat32m1_t __riscv_vfsqrt_v_f32m1(vfloat32m1_t vs2, size_t vl);
vfloat64m1_t __riscv_vfsqrt_v_f64m1(vfloat64m1_t vs2, size_t vl);
// (and LMUL variants m2, m4, m8, mf2, mf4, mf8)

// Masked
vfloat32m1_t __riscv_vfsqrt_v_f32m1_m(vbool32_t vm, vfloat32m1_t vs2, size_t vl);
```

No scalar version exists; use `fsqrt.s`/`fsqrt.d` in base F/D extensions for scalars.

The note points to the **vector floating-point reciprocal square-root estimate** instruction family: `vfrsqrt7`.

**Instruction:** `vfrsqrt7.vv` (only variant, no scalar form)

**Assembly format:**
```
vfrsqrt7.vv vd, vs2, vm
```
- `vd`: destination vector register (SEW-width FP elements, same SEW as source)
- `vs2`: source vector register of FP elements (SEW = {16,32,64})
- `vm`: vector mask (`v0.t` or omitted for unmasked)

**Behavior:** Computes a 7-bit accurate estimate of 1/√x for each active element. SEW must be FP16, FP32, or FP64. No scalar variant exists (the operation is inherently vector). Rounding mode and exceptions follow standard FP rules; the result is an **estimate** with guaranteed relative error ≤ 2⁻⁷.

**RVV C intrinsics:**
```c
// SEW=16: vfloat16m1_t, vfloat16m2_t, vfloat16m4_t, vfloat16m8_t
vfloat16m1_t __riscv_vfrsqrt7_v_f16m1(vfloat16m1_t vs2, size_t vl);
vfloat16m1_t __riscv_vfrsqrt7_v_f16m1_m(vbool16_t vm, vfloat16m1_t vs2, size_t vl);

// SEW=32: vfloat32m1_t, vfloat32m2_t, vfloat32m4_t, vfloat32m8_t
vfloat32m1_t __riscv_vfrsqrt7_v_f32m1(vfloat32m1_t vs2, size_t vl);
vfloat32m1_t __riscv_vfrsqrt7_v_f32m1_m(vbool32_t vm, vfloat32m1_t vs2, size_t vl);

// SEW=64: vfloat64m1_t, vfloat64m2_t, vfloat64m4_t, vfloat64m8_t
vfloat64m1_t __riscv_vfrsqrt7_v_f64m1(vfloat64m1_t vs2, size_t vl);
vfloat64m1_t __riscv_vfrsqrt7_v_f64m1_m(vbool64_t vm, vfloat64m1_t vs2, size_t vl);
```
Pattern: `__riscv_vfrsqrt7_v_f<SEW><LMUL>` and `__riscv_vfrsqrt7_v_f<SEW><LMUL>_m` for masked. No `.vx` (scalar) variant exists.

The instruction family is **VFREC7** (vector floating-point reciprocal 7-bit estimate).

**Instruction:** `vfrec7.v vd, vs2, vm`

**Assembly format:**
```
vfrec7.v vd, vs2, vm   # vector-vector: vd[i] = 1.0 / vs2[i] (estimate)
```

**Operand roles:**
- `vd`: destination vector register (floating-point)
- `vs2`: source vector register (floating-point)
- `vm`: vector mask (v0.t or omitted for unmasked)

**Behavior:** Computes a 7-bit accurate estimate of the reciprocal (1/x) for each active element. No scalar variant exists. Rounding mode is ignored; this is an estimate instruction with defined relative error ≤ 2⁻⁷. Sets invalid operation exception flag for ±0, ±∞, NaN inputs.

